#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119710 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d503d5d-ee8f-57a2-a6ef-b5b05d5e8ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Volume of a Rectangular Solid Given Its Dimensions"}] :question/id #uuid "78d0bc32-ce61-5c58-a31a-bf3b0cc55761" :question/math-academy-id "e-8972" :question/problem "What is the volume of a rectangular solid that has length, width, and height of $5 \\: \\textrm{m},$ $\\sqrt3 \\: \\textrm{m},$ and $\\sqrt6 \\: \\textrm{m},$ respectively?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=5 \\, \\textrm{m}, \\qquad w=\\sqrt3 \\, \\textrm{m}, \\qquad h=\\sqrt6 \\, \\textrm{m}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = 5 \\cdot \\sqrt3 \\cdot \\sqrt6 = 5\\sqrt{18} = 15\\sqrt2 \\: \\textrm{m}^3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119711 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119891 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a97dc9e3-b5a4-5913-94c3-aea43e639ce8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Dimension of a Rectangular Solid Given Its Volume and Some Other Dimensions"}] :question/id #uuid "35043b46-40e9-51c4-8676-ad7a81763aae" :question/math-academy-id "e-8973" :question/problem "Determine the height of the rectangular solid below given that its volume is $700\\:\\textrm{cm}^3.$\n\n![](images/8a/8a232f69c9c6abbffcb2272d4fbd237c644273826fb46dd5742bdc07d89544cb.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nV = 700, \\qquad l=14, \\qquad w=10\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}700 & =14\\cdot 10\\cdot h \\\\ 700 & =140h \\\\ h & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height is $5\\:\\textrm{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119712 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba5bf1d9-f6e2-5494-91fd-31c20d7fa09b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Rectangular Solid: Word Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "3528ee00-01a7-5c77-af81-50d53b5fa849" :question/math-academy-id "e-8974" :question/problem "The diagram below shows a carton of orange juice. One-third of the carton is filled with juice, forming a cube. Find the volume of the empty space in the carton.\n\n![](images/90/90280dd78d285df5dc798c3ec90b7fb47a72414f2934d36885eb9d65fd7584fd.png)" :question/worked-solution "The orange juice in the carton creates a cube. Since the height of the juice is $9\\,\\textrm{cm},$ the side length of the cube is also $9\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\nThe height of $9 \\: \\textrm{cm}$ shown in the diagram represents a third of the carton's height. Therefore, the height of the carton is\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot 9 = 27 \\: \\textrm{cm}.\n$$\n\nThe empty space in the carton is a rectangular prism that is $9\\:\\textrm{cm}$ long, $9\\:\\textrm{cm}$ wide, and $27 - 9 = 18\\:\\textrm{cm}$ high.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=9\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad w=9\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad h=18\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = 9 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 18 = 1\\,458\\:\\textrm{cm}^3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that there is $1\\,458 \\: \\textrm{cm}^3$ of empty space in the carton."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d503d5d-ee8f-57a2-a6ef-b5b05d5e8ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Volume of a Rectangular Solid Given Its Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12ce2424-f4f5-5c7a-9fd1-d519603ca18b" :